---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Privy vs Attentive for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Privy vs Attentive for Shopify stores — popup capture and basic email vs enterprise SMS-first personalization, stack architecture, and upgrade timing.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Privy or Attentive better for a Shopify store doing $200k/mo?',
    answer: 'They solve different problems — not either/or. Privy wins onsite capture and basic email for merchants under enterprise SMS contract economics. Attentive wins when SMS drives 25%+ of retention revenue with dedicated ownership and budget for six-figure annual contracts. At $200k/mo, Attentive is justified only if SMS ROI clearly covers contract minimums — Privy still owns capture regardless.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Attentive replace Privy for list growth?',
    answer: 'No. Attentive is SMS-first enterprise infrastructure — it assumes capture and email live elsewhere. Privy owns popups, exit intent, and gamification Attentive does not offer. Typical architecture at scale: Privy for capture and an email platform for lifecycle messaging, with Attentive for enterprise SMS. Attentive-only without capture tooling is a category error.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Privy vs Attentive pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Privy scales on sessions plus email sends — from ~$30/mo, predictable for SMB DTC. Attentive uses enterprise contracts keyed to list size, message volume, and feature package — often $1,500+/mo minimum for meaningful deployment. Privy is the economic default below $500k annual revenue unless SMS ROI clearly covers Attentive\'s floor.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use Privy and Attentive together?',
    answer: 'Yes, at enterprise SMS scale. Privy captures email and SMS opt-in at popup. Attentive owns personalized text journeys — drop alerts, conversational recovery, AI-assisted segmentation. Critical: sync suppression across Privy capture, email ESP, and Attentive SMS. Enterprise stacks fail on duplicate messaging, not vendor count.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better TCPA compliance tooling?',
    answer: 'Attentive leads — keyword management, legal review workflows, quiet hours, and compliance dashboards are built for enterprise SMS operators. Privy SMS capture exists but compliance depth is capture-oriented. If legal counsel reviews SMS programs quarterly and SMS is a primary revenue channel, Attentive\'s guardrails justify consideration over Privy SMS basics.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles cart abandonment better?',
    answer: 'Depends on channel and scale. Privy email cart recovery is adequate for early-stage stores. Attentive SMS cart recovery wins on immediacy and personalization for mobile-first flash drops at enterprise volume — athletic wear, limited releases. Best results combine Privy capture, email ESP cart flows, and Attentive SMS — not Privy email alone at $200k/mo.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'If evaluating capture: run Privy popup A/B — Attentive is not in that race. If evaluating enterprise SMS: run keyword opt-in, conversational cart recovery, drop alerts with holdout testing, and BFCM broadcast with legal review. Attentive should win SMS-specific tests at scale. Privy remains capture owner regardless of Attentive adoption timing.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Privy vs Attentive</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Attentive for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          SMB capture infrastructure versus enterprise SMS personalization. Privy lives on your storefront — popups, spin wheels, and basic automations for merchants turning sessions into subscribers. Attentive lives in enterprise text programs — AI-assisted segmentation, legal review workflows, and drop alerts for brands where SMS is a primary revenue channel. We evaluated both through a $210k/mo athletic wear label on Privy capture plus Klaviyo email plus Attentive SMS, a $165k/mo beauty brand that evaluated Attentive before capture was solved, and a $88k/mo home fitness equipment shop correctly deferring Attentive until SMS revenue crossed internal thresholds.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Popup capture', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Exit intent, gamification, and session-to-subscriber at SMB scale.' },
            { label: 'Enterprise SMS', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Personalization, compliance, and scale for text-first brands.' },
            { label: 'Early-stage email', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Basic welcome and cart before ESP graduation.' },
            { label: 'SMS revenue focus', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Built for operators who treat text as a primary channel.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Privy</th><th>Attentive</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Capture + basic email</td><td>Enterprise SMS-first</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$30/mo</td><td>Enterprise contracts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>Sessions + email sends</td><td>Contract + usage tiers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Popups & capture</td><td>Core strength</td><td>Not primary — pair required</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email automation</td><td>Basic welcome, cart</td><td>Secondary — pair required</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS depth</td><td>Capture-tied basics</td><td>Best-in-class at scale</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>Capture-oriented</td><td>Enterprise-grade</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical role</td><td>Top-of-funnel capture</td><td>SMS layer in combined stack</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          Merchants search "Privy vs Attentive" when enterprise peers name-drop Attentive at conferences or when SMS revenue crosses 20% of attributable retention. The comparison is a category mismatch — Privy solves capture at SMB economics; Attentive solves SMS personalization at enterprise economics. Treating Attentive as a Privy replacement or Privy as an Attentive alternative wastes evaluation time both vendors would admit in honest sales calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          The athletic wear label at $210k/mo treated SMS as 34% of attributable retention revenue. Attentive's drop alerts, holdout testing, and conversational cart recovery justified dedicated SMS ownership alongside Klaviyo email. Privy still owned capture — gamified popups during limited drops converted 5.1% of sessions. Removing Privy to fund Attentive would have shrunk the top of funnel Attentive's SMS program depended on.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: athletic wear enterprise stack</h2>
        <p>
          Privy captured email and SMS opt-in with compliant dual consent during hyped releases. Klaviyo owned email welcome, browse abandonment, and post-purchase. Attentive owned text drop alerts firing 90 minutes before public sale with personalized product recommendations from browse history. Legal reviewed Attentive keyword campaigns quarterly — workflow Privy's capture-oriented SMS never supported.
        </p>
        <p>
          Suppression sync across three tools required documented rules and a dedicated retention ops contractor. Enterprise stack success is architecture and ownership — not vendor count alone.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: beauty brand evaluating Attentive too early</h2>
        <p>
          At $165k/mo this brand received an Attentive enterprise pitch after a peer recommendation. Capture rate was 1.4% — binding constraint was sessions-to-subscribers, not SMS personalization depth. Attentive demo impressed; contract minimum did not pass CFO review when capture remained unsolved.
        </p>
        <p>
          They invested in Privy popup optimization first — capture rose to 3.2% over eight weeks. Attentive re-entered evaluation when SMS revenue crossed 18% six months later. The email platform remained a separate lifecycle decision so the team could fund Attentive without forcing capture and messaging into one product. Sequence mattered: capture before enterprise SMS.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: home fitness deferring Attentive</h2>
        <p>
          At $88k/mo this equipment brand ran Privy-only with adequate email cart recovery. SMS drove 9% of retention revenue — below their 15% threshold for Attentive or Postscript addition. They modeled Attentive contract floor at $1,800/mo against current SMS-attributed revenue of $7,900/mo — 23% of channel revenue consumed by platform minimum before message fees.
        </p>
        <p>
          Decision: optimize Privy capture during New Year resolution traffic, add Postscript if SMS crossed 12%, defer Attentive until $150k/mo revenue with 20%+ SMS share. Discipline over conference FOMO.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: popup capture with SMS opt-in</h2>
        <p>
          Privy dual opt-in popup during the athletic wear label's spring drop — email plus SMS with compliant checkbox — lifted combined capture 3.8% to 5.1%. Attentive consumed opt-ins Privy collected; it did not replace Privy's onsite conversion tooling. Capture tests run in Privy; SMS journey tests run in Attentive after opt-in exists.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart via SMS at enterprise scale</h2>
        <p>
          Privy email cart recovery recovered 8% of abandoned carts for home fitness at $340 AOV — acceptable for early stage. Attentive SMS cart recovery on the athletic wear label added conversational replies and dynamic product recommendations — incremental 6% recovery on mobile abandoners email missed, with holdout testing proving lift was not vanity attribution.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality: SMB capture vs enterprise SMS</h2>
        <p>
          Privy session billing spikes during product drops — budget peak month, still predictable at SMB scale. Attentive contract minimums gate adoption timing — not capability. Combined Privy plus Attentive plus email ESP is expensive by design; justify with channel-attributed revenue, not stack minimalism fantasies.
        </p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Privy if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Capture rate is under 2.5% regardless of Attentive interest</li>
          <li>You need popups plus basic email at SMB economics</li>
          <li>SMS revenue is below 15–20% of retention attribution</li>
          <li>Enterprise SMS contract minimums fail CFO review today</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Attentive if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Capture is solved and SMS is a primary retention channel</li>
          <li>You have dedicated SMS ownership and legal review capacity</li>
          <li>SMS drives 25%+ of attributable retention revenue</li>
          <li>You will keep Privy for capture alongside Attentive SMS</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>90-day rollout: capture first, enterprise SMS later</h2>
        <p>
          Weeks 1–4: Privy live on theme — popup, exit intent, SMS capture with TCPA-compliant copy. Measure list growth rate and cost per email subscriber before any Attentive conversation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Weeks 5–8: Model Attentive contract economics against SMS revenue — enterprise minimums often require $150k+ monthly store revenue and six-figure annual SMS send projections.
        </p>
        <p>
          Weeks 9–12: If below Attentive threshold, deploy Postscript or Omnisend SMS with Privy capture. Revisit Attentive when SMS exceeds 20% of retention revenue and dedicated SMS ops hire exists.
        </p>

        <h2>Margin math: Privy capture ROI versus Attentive minimums</h2>
        <p>
          Privy at $45/mo capturing 800 emails monthly at $2.80 CPA delivers list growth Klaviyo or Attentive cannot monetize without subscribers. Attentive contracts often start $1k+/mo — justified when SMS drives $15k+ monthly attributed revenue. Home fitness brand deferring Attentive saved $12k annually while Privy plus Postscript handled capture and text profitably at $38k/mo revenue.
        </p>

        <h2>Failure rehearsal: stack timing mistakes</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Attentive before capture infrastructure.</strong> Enterprise SMS with weak opt-in volume — contract minimums burn budget without list to message.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Privy as full lifecycle ESP.</strong> Capture tool cannot replace welcome, cart, and winback depth — email layer still required.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: beauty brand evaluating Attentive too early</h2>
        <p>
          The $42k/mo beauty brand received Attentive outbound at $1.2k/mo minimum — SMS revenue projected at $4k monthly could not justify contract until store crossed $85k/mo. Privy capture plus Postscript SMS delivered similar architecture at $220/mo combined. They documented Attentive re-evaluation trigger: 25% retention revenue from SMS plus dedicated lifecycle hire.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write your Attentive re-evaluation trigger at Privy install — revenue threshold, SMS attribution percentage, and headcount requirement — so enterprise sales outreach does not compress stack decisions prematurely.
        </p>


        <h2>Operational depth: Attentive re-evaluation trigger document</h2>
        <p>
          Write at Privy install: revenue threshold ($85k–$150k depending on margin), SMS attribution floor (20–25%), headcount requirement (dedicated lifecycle or SMS ops). Beauty brand at $42k/mo correctly rejected $1.2k/mo minimum — Postscript plus Privy at $220/mo sufficed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Athletic wear enterprise stack succeeded with quarterly legal review of Attentive keywords and documented suppression across Privy, Klaviyo, Attentive — architecture and ownership, not vendor count alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Capture constraint binding at 1.4% session rate — Attentive demo is distraction until Privy optimization lifts capture above 3%. Enterprise SMS cannot fix funnel top leakage; beauty brand proved sequence by fixing capture first, Attentive six months later.
        </p>
        <p>
          Home fitness modeled Attentive floor consuming 23% of SMS revenue before message fees — deferral until $150k/mo and 20% SMS share. Write the model in spreadsheet at Privy signup so outbound enterprise sales does not compress decisions.
        </p>


        <h2>Weekly operator checklist during trial</h2>
        <p>
          For Privy versus Attentive, hold a fixed trial rhythm: Monday checks Shopify-to-Privy and Shopify-to-Attentive latency against the ten-minute suppression threshold; Wednesday exercises gift/subscription/financing edge cases in Privy and Attentive alike; Friday reprices the Privy versus Attentive year-one model from actuals — contacts punish hoarding, sends punish over-mailing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whichever side of Privy versus Attentive maps to this quarter’s constraint deserves the vote — Privy for depth-limited teams, Attentive for bandwidth-limited ones — provided the edge holds without vendor rescue at peak. The Privy versus Attentive merchant anecdote above shows one shape of that tradeoff; verify it on your numbers before adopting it.
        </p>
        <h2>Pre-decision audit</h2>
        <p>
          Don't annual-commit Privy versus Attentive on reading alone — complete scored trials in Privy and Attentive, project peak Privy versus Attentive sends, confirm tag/CRM needs, clean inactives before contact-priced quotes, and stage kill-switches for a hybrid Privy/Attentive window. Cited Privy versus Attentive outcomes guide; your measured trial concludes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Privy versus Attentive verdict belongs to the calmer Thursday edit: whichever of Privy or Attentive ships suppression and VIP access fastest wins on revenue and margin.
        </p>

        <h2>Sale-week edit safety gate</h2>
        <p>
          The decisive tiebreaker between Privy and Attentive is rarely feature depth — it is whether a non-technical marketer can add recent-purchaser suppression and VIP early access in under thirty minutes on a Thursday before a drop. Run that edit in both trials before annual commit. Platforms that pass calm-week demos but fail sale-week edits cost more in foregone peak revenue than any annual subscription delta.
        </p>
        <p>
          Document trial scores in writing: welcome time-to-live, cart suppression accuracy, post-purchase edge cases, winback engagement splits, pricing at 12-month list size, team confidence without support tickets. Scores within three points mean pick lower migration risk. Scores diverging ten or more points mean migrate decisively — middle paths keep duplicate-send risk alive.
        </p>
        <p>
          Minimum viable trial length remains 90 days with weekly operator checklist — shorter trials miss billing surprises and peak-season behavior both Privy and Attentive surface only under operational stress. Baseline metrics in week one make before-and-after claims credible to finance.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Log Privy versus Attentive baselines — welcome, cart, post-purchase, winback on Privy and Attentive — in week one; Privy versus Attentive before-and-after claims without them fail finance review. Revisit the Privy-versus-Attentive call every ninety days as mix and volume drift.</em>
        </p>

        <h2>Failure rehearsal: renewal-season panic migration</h2>
        <p>
          Teams rediscover comparison pages only at contract renewal under peak-season pressure — worst time to migrate. Schedule evaluation 120 days before renewal or BFCM, run four-flow trial calmly, and keep kill-switch spreadsheet ready. Panic migration causes duplicate cart recovery and deliverability dips that take quarters to heal.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pair comparison verdict with one related store-type guide and one use-case playbook linked at page bottom — workflow tests in trials matter more when catalog shape and primary bottleneck are named explicitly in your internal rollout doc.
        </p>
        <p>Minimum 90-day trial with documented four-flow scores before renewal — shorter evaluations miss peak-season billing surprises and edit-safety failures that determine real-world platform fit.</p>

        <h2>Enterprise SMS minimum versus Privy capture economics</h2>
        <p>
          Streetwear brand: Privy drop waitlist popups 4.1% capture; Attentive SMS contract $1,500/mo floor before email foundation mature. Architecture: Privy capture plus Postscript SMS until order volume justifies Attentive. Attentive trial before capture solved wastes procurement hours — capture under 2% makes Privy non-negotiable regardless of SMS vendor ambition.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow validation: privy vs attentive</h2>
        <p>
          Write Privy versus Attentive trial scores down — welcome latency, cart suppression, gift-buyer edge, winback segmentation, priced-at-scale cost for Privy and Attentive — and use the half-hour sale-week edit as the Privy versus Attentive tiebreaker over demo aesthetics.
        </p>
        <p>
          Privy versus Attentive needs a full quarter: weekly sync/edge-case/pricing checks across Privy and Attentive, baselines captured in week one. Anything shorter misleads finance on Privy-versus-Attentive billing and overlap behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Privy wins capture at every revenue band; Attentive wins enterprise SMS when contract economics pass CFO review. Solve capture first with Privy; add Attentive when SMS revenue and volume justify enterprise infrastructure. Never treat Attentive as a Privy replacement. The winner is stack timing — not a single vendor pick at opposite ends of the funnel.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive">Omnisend vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/privy-vs-postscript">Privy vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
